Under Singapore law, the doctrine of economic duress addresses situations where one party threatens to breach a contract with a business partner to coerce the other into a new or modified agreement.

The leading authority is E C Investment Holding Pte Ltd v Ridout Residence Pte Ltd [2011] 2 SLR 232, where the court considered whether a threat to breach a contract constituted duress. The court applied the test for economic duress established in Pao On v Lau Yiu Long [1980] AC 614 and developed in Universe Tankships Inc of Monrovia v International Transport Workers Federation [1983] 1 AC 366.

In Third World Development Ltd v Atang Latief [1990] 1 SLR(R) 96, the Singapore court recognised that a threat to break a contract can constitute illegitimate pressure amounting to duress, rendering the resulting agreement voidable.

The elements of economic duress in Singapore, as confirmed in Sharon Global Solutions Pte Ltd v LG International (Singapore) Pte Ltd [2001] 2 SLR(R) 233, are:
(1) Pressure amounting to compulsion of the victim's will (not merely commercial pressure);
(2) The illegitimacy of the pressure exerted;
(3) A causal link between the pressure and the contract entered into;
(4) The absence of a reasonable alternative open to the victim.

A threat to breach a contract is considered illegitimate pressure. However, mere commercial pressure or hard bargaining is not sufficient — the pressure must vitiate consent. The court considers whether the victim had any practical choice but to submit to the demand.

Under the Rules of Court 2021 (ROC 2021), the provision governing originating processes to be served out of Singapore (i.e., service out of jurisdiction) is found in Order 8 Rule 1.

Order 8 Rule 1 of the ROC 2021 provides that an originating process may be served out of Singapore with the leave of the court in certain specified circumstances, including where:
(a) the claim is founded on a cause of action arising within Singapore;
(b) the claim relates to a contract made within Singapore, or governed by Singapore law, or containing a term to the effect that the Singapore courts shall have jurisdiction;
(c) the claim is in respect of a breach of contract committed within Singapore;
(d) the claim is founded on a tort committed within Singapore;
(e) the claim relates to property within Singapore;
(f) other specified grounds.

Under the former Rules of Court (Cap 322, R 5):
- Order 11 Rule 1 governed service out of jurisdiction and set out the grounds upon which leave could be obtained.
- Order 11 Rule 2 required the application for leave to be supported by an affidavit.

The test for obtaining leave to serve out of jurisdiction was stated in Siemens AG v Holdrich Investment Ltd [2010] 3 SLR 1007:
(1) There is a good arguable case that the claim falls within one of the heads in O 11 r 1 (now O 8 r 1);
(2) There is a serious issue to be tried on the merits;
(3) Singapore is the forum conveniens — the most appropriate forum for the trial of the action.

Under Singapore law, a defendant who successfully defeats an ordinary civil action may subsequently claim costs from the unsuccessful claimant. The question appears to ask whether a successful defendant can bring a subsequent action for malicious prosecution (of civil proceedings) or abuse of process.

The position in Singapore is:

(1) Costs: The successful defendant is ordinarily entitled to costs on the standard basis under O 21 of the Rules of Court 2021. Indemnity costs may be awarded where the claim was brought unreasonably or in bad faith.

(2) Malicious prosecution of civil proceedings: The tort of malicious prosecution applies in Singapore. In Lee Tat Development Pte Ltd v MCST Plan No 301 [2018] 2 SLR 866, the Court of Appeal affirmed that the elements of malicious prosecution of civil proceedings are:
   (a) The plaintiff must have prosecuted the earlier proceedings against the defendant;
   (b) The proceedings terminated in favour of the defendant;
   (c) The proceedings were initiated without reasonable and probable cause;
   (d) The proceedings were initiated with malice (an improper purpose);
   (e) The defendant suffered damage.

(3) Abuse of process: Independently of malicious prosecution, a party may be liable for abuse of process if the proceedings were commenced for an improper collateral purpose.

The successful defendant therefore has the option of: (a) claiming costs in the original action; and (b) bringing a fresh action for malicious prosecution of civil proceedings if the elements of that tort can be established.

Under Singapore law, the common-law jurisdiction to pierce the corporate veil is confined to narrow circumstances, principally the "evasion principle" derived from Prest v Petrodel Resources Ltd [2013] UKSC 34.

The Court of Appeal in Alwie Handoyo v Tjong Very Sumito [2013] SGCA 44 at [96] affirmed that the corporate veil may be pierced in Singapore, but only in limited circumstances. The court endorsed the approach in Prest v Petrodel, which distinguishes between:

(1) The concealment principle: Where the corporate structure is used to conceal the true facts — here, the court does not pierce the veil but simply looks behind the corporate façade to identify the true facts. No legal principle prevents the court from finding facts.

(2) The evasion principle: Where a person is under an existing legal obligation or restriction which he deliberately evades or whose enforcement he deliberately frustrates by interposing a company under his control — in this narrow case, the court may pierce the corporate veil and hold the controller liable.

The key elements of the evasion principle are:
(a) There must be a pre-existing legal obligation, liability, or restriction on the controller;
(b) The controller must have deliberately interposed the company to evade that obligation;
(c) The company must be under the controller's effective control;
(d) There must be impropriety — mere use of a corporate structure for legitimate commercial reasons is not sufficient.

The Court of Appeal has emphasised that piercing the corporate veil remains a remedy of last resort — other legal mechanisms (agency, trusts, sham, attribution) should be considered first. In NEC Asia Pte Ltd v Picket & Rail Asia Pacific Pte Ltd [2011] 2 SLR 565, the High Court similarly confined veil-piercing to the evasion category.

Under Singapore law, the doctrine of legitimate expectations in administrative law recognises that where a public authority makes a clear and unqualified representation to an individual, the authority may be held to that representation.

The leading case is Chiu Teng @ Kallang Pte Ltd v Singapore Land Authority [2014] 1 SLR 1047, where the High Court applied the doctrine of substantive legitimate expectations. The court held that for a legitimate expectation to arise:
(1) There must be a clear, unambiguous, and unqualified representation or established practice by the public authority;
(2) The representation must be made to the applicant (or a defined class to which the applicant belongs);
(3) The applicant must have relied on the representation (reliance may be presumed in some cases);
(4) It would be unconscionable or unfair for the authority to resile from the representation without adequate justification.

In SGB Starkstrom Pte Ltd v Commissioner for Labour [2016] 3 SLR 598, the Court of Appeal discussed the availability of substantive legitimate expectations in Singapore, noting that while procedural legitimate expectations are well-established, the availability of substantive legitimate expectations (i.e., entitling the individual to the substantive benefit promised) remains subject to debate.

The critical limitation is that even where a legitimate expectation arises, it can be overridden by the public authority if there is an overriding public interest justification for departing from the representation. The authority must show that the departure is justified and that the individual was given an opportunity to make representations before the change.

Yes, NFTs (non-fungible tokens) constitute property under Singapore law, notwithstanding the argument that information per se does not constitute property.

The definitive authority is Janesh s/o Rajkumar v Unknown Person ("CHEFPIERRE") [2022] SGHC 264, where the High Court expressly held that an NFT constitutes property capable of being the subject of a proprietary injunction. The court applied the criteria from National Provincial Bank v Ainsworth [1965] AC 1175, holding that an NFT:
(a) is definable (it has a unique identifier on the blockchain);
(b) is identifiable by third parties (the blockchain is a public, transparent ledger);
(c) is capable in its nature of assumption by third parties (it can be transferred);
(d) has some degree of permanence or stability (the blockchain record is immutable).

The court distinguished the general proposition that "information is not property" (as stated in Oxford v Moss [1979] Crim LR 119) on the basis that an NFT is not mere information — it is a unique digital token with economic value, transferability, and exclusivity. The token itself (rather than the underlying digital content it references) is the property.

This builds on the earlier recognition of cryptocurrency as property in B2C2 Ltd v Quoine Pte Ltd [2019] SGHC(I) 3 at [142] and CLM v CLN [2022] SGHC 46 at [40]–[46]. The rationale is that crypto-assets (including NFTs) satisfy the functional requirements of property even though they are intangible and exist purely as entries on a distributed ledger.

The conceptual difficulty raised by "information is not property" does not defeat the claim because an NFT is more than information — it represents a unique, scarce, transferable digital asset with defined ownership rights enforced by the blockchain protocol.

The application of the objective "reasonable man" test to determine contract formation is well-established in Singapore law. However, it is important to note that Singapore does not have the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (UK) — Singapore has its own Unfair Contract Terms Act (Cap 396) (UCTA), which is modelled on the UK Act but adapted for Singapore.

The objective test for contract formation in Singapore:

(1) The Court of Appeal in Gay Choon Ing v Loh Sze Ti Terence Peter [2009] 2 SLR(R) 332 affirmed that contract formation is assessed objectively — the court looks at what a reasonable person in the position of the parties would have understood the communications to mean, not at the parties' subjective intentions.

(2) The test is: would a reasonable person in the position of the offeree have understood the offeror's words and conduct as constituting an offer (or acceptance)?

(3) Under UCTA (Cap 396), the reasonableness test under s 11 determines whether an exclusion or limitation clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness. Schedule 2 sets out guidelines for applying the reasonableness test, including:
   (a) the relative bargaining positions of the parties;
   (b) whether the customer received an inducement to agree to the term;
   (c) whether the customer knew or ought to have known of the term;
   (d) whether goods were manufactured to the customer's special order.

The "reasonable man" in the context of UCTA does not determine contract formation — it determines whether a particular contractual term (typically an exclusion clause) is enforceable. These are distinct inquiries: formation is assessed by the objective test from Gay Choon Ing; enforceability of terms is assessed under UCTA's reasonableness standard.

Under the Copyright Act 2021 (No 22 of 2021), the statutory fair use provisions are set out in s 190, which enacts a general fair use exception (replacing the former fair dealing provisions under the Copyright Act (Cap 63)).

Section 190 of the Copyright Act 2021 provides that a use of a copyrighted work is fair use if it is fair, having regard to the following factors:

(1) The purpose and character of the use, including whether the use is of a commercial nature or is for non-profit educational purposes (s 190(2)(a));

(2) The nature of the copyrighted work or protected performance (s 190(2)(b));

(3) The amount and substantiality of the part used in relation to the whole work or performance (s 190(2)(c));

(4) The effect of the use upon the potential market for, or value of, the work or performance (s 190(2)(d));

(5) The possibility of obtaining the work or performance, or an equivalent work or performance, within a reasonable time at an ordinary commercial price (s 190(2)(e)).

This five-factor test is notably broader than the US fair use defence (which has four factors under 17 USC § 107) and the former Singapore fair dealing provisions. The fifth factor (availability at a reasonable price) is unique to Singapore and reflects the Singapore Parliament's concern with access to works.

Key differences from the former regime:
- The former Copyright Act (Cap 63) provided specific fair dealing exceptions for research/study (s 35), criticism/review (s 36), and news reporting (s 37).
- The 2021 Act adopts an open-ended fair use defence, not limited to specific purposes.
- The five factors are non-exhaustive — the court may consider other relevant factors.

Under Singapore law, issue estoppel prevents a party from relitigating an issue that has already been decided against it in earlier proceedings. The doctrine is well-established and was comprehensively restated by the Court of Appeal.

The leading authority is Lee Tat Development Pte Ltd v MCST Plan No 301 [2005] 3 SLR(R) 157, where the Court of Appeal set out the four requirements for issue estoppel:
(1) There must be a final and conclusive judgment on the merits by a court of competent jurisdiction;
(2) The judgment must be between the same parties (or their privies);
(3) There must be identity of subject matter in the two proceedings — specifically, the issue decided in the earlier proceedings must be identical to the issue raised in the later proceedings;
(4) The issue must have been raised, argued, and actually decided in the earlier proceedings (i.e., the finding must have been necessary for the earlier decision and not merely obiter).

The Court of Appeal in Wing Joo Loong Ginseng Hong (Singapore) Co Pte Ltd v Qinghai Xinyuan Foreign Trade Co Ltd [2009] 2 SLR(R) 814 further clarified that issue estoppel applies even if the earlier decision was on a different cause of action — what matters is that the specific issue was determined.

Additionally, the "extended" doctrine of res judicata (or the rule in Henderson v Henderson (1843) 3 Hare 100) prevents a party from raising in subsequent proceedings matters which were not but could and should have been raised in the earlier proceedings (Goh Nellie v Goh Lian Teck [2007] 1 SLR(R) 453).

Issue estoppel may be departed from in "special circumstances" where it would result in injustice (Arnold v NatWest Bank plc [1991] 2 AC 93, applied in Singapore), but this exception is narrow.
